  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/tympanopleura-cryptica-4/",
      "html": "<p>A small dolphin catfish imported from Peru for the first time. The largest scientifically known specimen had a standard length (without the tail fin) of barely 8.5 cm. Tympanopleura cryptica was described scientifically only in 2015.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/tympanopleura-cryptica-4/",
      "html": "<p>It occurs in the upper Amazon, where the river is still called the Solimões.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/tympanopleura-cryptica-4/",
      "html": "<p>The catfish accept all usual frozen food. They are predatory and can eat surprisingly large fish.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/tympanopleura-cryptica-4/",
      "html": "<p>Tympanopleura are predatory fish that can eat surprisingly large fish, so care should be taken with tankmates.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/tympanopleura-cryptica-4/",
      "html": "<p>During the reproductive period, males of T. cryptica, like all dolphin catfish, get a strongly extended dorsal fin spine that serves during mating to press the female against the side of the male. Internal fertilization takes place with a mating organ formed from the first five rays of the anal fin, by which males can also be recognized outside the reproductive period, while the long dorsal fin spine is shed. This can be compared to the antlers of stags, which are also formed anew each year.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/tympanopleura-cryptica-4/",
      "html": "<p>The species name cryptica means \"hidden, concealed\": before its description the species was probably always confused with other species. The very large nose pores at first suggested the animals had bumped into something, but since this occurs in many specimens, always on both sides, and is also seen in the preserved type specimens, it is considered normal.</p>"
    }
  ],
